(2.26) \[a_{1}(\mathbf{u};\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v})=\int_{\Omega}\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{u}(u_{1}v_{2}-u_{2}v_{1})\,dx\] \[\forall\mathbf{u}=(u_{1},u_{2})\!\in\!H_{0}^{1}(\Omega)^{2},\quad \quad\mathbf{v}=(v_{1},v_{2})\!\in\!V.\]

This result stems from the identities:

(2.27) \[\left\{\begin{aligned} u_{1}\frac{\partial u_{1}}{ \partial x_{1}}+u_{2}\frac{\partial u_{1}}{\partial x_{2}}&=(1/2 )\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{1}}(u_{1}^{2}+u_{2}^{2})-u_{2}\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{u},\\ u_{1}\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial x_{1}}+u_{2}\frac{\partial u_ {2}}{\partial x_{2}}&=(1/2)\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{2}}(u_{1} ^{2}+u_{2}^{2})+u_{1}\operatorname{curl}\mathbf{u},\end{aligned}\right.\]

followed by an integration by parts to eliminate \(\mathbf{grad}(\|\mathbf{u}\|^{2})\). Therefore the nonlinear term reads:

(2.28) \[a_{1}(\mathbf{u};\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v})=\int_{\Omega}\mathcal{A} \psi\bigg{(}\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial x_{2}}\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x _{1}}-\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial x_{1}}\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x_{2}} \bigg{)}dx\] \[\forall\mathbf{u}=\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi,\quad\quad \mathbf{v}=\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\phi\quad\text{with }\psi,\phi\quad\text{in }\Psi.\]

